---
import EngineSketch from "../../../components/studies/engines/EngineEffects.svelte";
import PlumeLifecycle from "../../../components/studies/engines/PlumeLifecycle.svelte";
import TailComparison from "../../../components/studies/engines/TailComparison.svelte";
import ThrustPlume from "../../../components/studies/engines/ThrustPlume.svelte";
import { studies } from "../../../data/studies";
import StudyLayout from "../../../layouts/StudyLayout.astro";

const study = studies.find((item) => item.slug === "engine-effects");
---

<StudyLayout study={study}>
	<p>
		<strong>Approved · 30 October 2026.</strong>
		The owner accepted this alpha study and its implementation plan, and
		authorized implementation after the study is committed and pushed. The
		values remain provisional; approval does not claim completed implementation.
	</p>
	<p>
		<strong>Alpha implementation · 30 October 2026.</strong>
		The approved plan is now implemented locally in the shared game and
		<a href="/lab/flight">flight Lab</a>: complete fading plumes, output-based
		size, and the provisional 750 directional cutoff. The sections below
		preserve the reasoning and pre-implementation assessment. Full-scene human
		feel and device performance remain open.
	</p>
	<section aria-labelledby="engine-purpose">
		<h2 id="engine-purpose">01 / Flying as a graphic effect</h2>
		<p>
			The visual reference is the late-nineties and early-two-thousands vector
			art and effects era described in the brief: flashy, graphic, and sharply
			composed. Here, “vector” means surfaces, glows, interpolation, and
			effects, not a collection of lines pointing in different directions.
			Simple forms can produce a rich spectacle through their shape, overlap,
			light, and transformation. Simplicity belongs to the visual vocabulary; it
			should not drain the result of energy.
		</p>
		<p>
			Press one engine, add the second, then release both and drift. The
			ambition is for that ordinary action to become a small graphic performance
			on the ship and across the field. A possible direction is luminous
			surfaces that swell, stretch, blend, and dissolve as the manoeuvre
			develops. That is a hypothesis to explore, not a chosen effect. The
			current shared flame and uniform position trace give us a functional
			starting point, but do not establish the aesthetic we are aiming for.
		</p>
		<p>
			The design problem remains: make engine activity immediate and leave a
			playful, fading memory of flight. Its visual ambition now leads the
			inquiry. We need to examine silhouette, filled area, gradients, glow, and
			transitions together, including how effects overlap the hull and persist
			in world space. That composition should inform later rendering layers and
			implementation. The exact reference within this broad era still needs a
			shared visual basis.
		</p>
		<p>
			The study now moves into exploration with this aesthetic framing,
			following the discussion of surfaces, glow, and interpolation.
		</p>
	</section>
	<section aria-labelledby="engine-next">
		<h2 id="engine-next">02.1 / One surface, three layers</h2>
		<p>
			The smallest hypothesis: a filled, curved plume with a bright core and
			soft glow can provide enough graphic richness. Repeated impressions of
			that surface remain in space, spread, and fade over 1.5 seconds. Toggle
			glow to see how much comes from the shape and how much comes from light.
		</p>
		<EngineSketch client:load />
		<p>
			This sketch uses the production balanced ship, production engine physics,
			and ship drawing: one second of left engine, two of both, then three of
			release. The display follows the ship at a fixed enlarged scale. Gravity,
			hazards, and arena wrapping are omitted to isolate the effects; engine
			attachment positions, palette, and plumes are study proposals. This tests
			visual character, not full-scene readability, final camera behaviour, or
			performance on a phone.
		</p>
		<p>
			<strong>Accepted visual direction · 30 October 2026.</strong>
			The owner judged the style a substantial improvement and a match for the
			intended aesthetic: glowing surfaces left behind and vanishing over time.
			Keep this example as the reference for that quality. The straight-back
			engine orientation is incomplete; exact colour, timing, and geometry have
			not been settled, and this is not approval for production integration.
		</p>
	</section>
	<section aria-labelledby="engine-direction">
		<h2 id="engine-direction">
			02.2 / A continuous tail through straight flight and turns
		</h2>
		<p>
			The angled plumes read better, but the owner found that the residual
			impressions neither follow the turn convincingly nor combine into a
			unified flame in straight flight. The glowing surface style remains the
			foundation; the tail is unresolved. This is a revision of the same
			investigation.
		</p>
		<p>
			This trial joins the emission history into a continuous, tapering surface.
			Both engines feed one central tail; the turning engine feeds an angled
			tail. The attached 12° plumes retain their previous shape, palette, and
			glow.
		</p>
		<TailComparison client:load />
		<p>
			Both views use production balanced-ship engine physics and drawing,
			starting at rest. Left continuously fires both engines; right continuously
			fires one. A fixed-scale camera follows each ship without rotating.
			Gravity, hazards, and wrapping are omitted by recentering the physics
			fixture each tick. Residue moves backward at a proposed 70 units/s and
			fades over 1.2 s; a distance fade over 145 units keeps the end visible
			during acceleration. These are visual trial values. The joined central
			tail is an artistic proposal, not a new force model. This does not
			establish full-arena readability or phone performance.
		</p>
		<p>
			<strong>Unresolved · preserved for comparison.</strong>
			The owner found this better, but release exposes a flat cutoff on the
			residue while the attached flame disappears instantly. The ribbon
			construction feels overcomplicated and does not yet deliver the intended
			arcade effect. No tail solution was accepted here; 02.3 investigates a
			different rendering model.
		</p>
		<details>
			<summary>Previous 02.2 reference · separate fading impressions</summary>
			<p>
				Preserved for comparison: the angled plumes were liked; their stamped
				residue was not accepted.
			</p>
			<EngineSketch
				client:visible
				splayDegrees={12}
				label="Previous angled plume and separate residue reference"
			/>
		</details>
	</section>
	<section aria-labelledby="plume-lifecycle">
		<h2 id="plume-lifecycle">02.3 / The plume itself lives on</h2>
		<p>
			The previous model draws a flame attached to the ship and a separate
			ribbon behind it. This trial removes that split. Each engine emits
			complete, softly bounded glowing surfaces. They overlap into the flame,
			drift, expand, and fade. Release stops new emission; even the newest
			surface keeps its remaining lifetime, so there is no flame object to
			switch off or ribbon to cut.
		</p>
		<PlumeLifecycle client:load />
		<p>
			Same straight and turning physics fixture and 12° engine directions as
			02.2. The new surfaces have rounded, transparent boundaries and live for
			0.65 seconds, retaining 80% of ship velocity at emission plus a backward
			drift of 45 units/s. These are trial values, not accepted tuning. There is
			no connected strip or distance cutoff. This tests motion and shutdown;
			full-scene readability and physical-phone performance remain untested.
		</p>
		<p>
			<strong>Accepted · 30 October 2026.</strong>
			The owner confirmed this is the intended plume lifecycle. Preserve
			complete emitted surfaces that remain alive after release, then drift and
			fade. The next inquiry concerns sizing each engine’s plume by the thrust
			it contributes.
		</p>
	</section>
	<section aria-labelledby="thrust-plume">
		<h2 id="thrust-plume">02.4 / Size follows delivered thrust</h2>
		<p>
			The accepted plume lifecycle stays intact. This trial asks whether the
			amount of visible flame can explain what each engine contributes on a
			simulation step. Here “power” means delivered thrust, not the ship’s
			stored energy resource or total speed.
		</p>
		<p>
			<strong>Owner direction · 30 October 2026:</strong>
			engines should add no further thrust at roughly 750 units/s in the
			direction they push. This trial uses 750 as the provisional cutoff, with a
			simple linear taper from full contribution at rest to zero there. The
			cutoff intent is agreed; the precise value and taper shape remain tuning
			proposals. Already-faster motion is preserved. Braking and sideways thrust
			remain available.
		</p>
		<ThrustPlume client:load />
		<p>
			The fixture measures the velocity impulse actually applied by the engine
			update with the study-local cutoff and divides it between active engines.
			Proposed mapping: plume area is proportional to that engine’s fraction of
			full thrust, so length and width each scale by its square root. Each
			surface keeps its emission size throughout its accepted fade. The
			straight-flight, hazard-free fixture starts at rest; choose a starting
			speed and restart to compare. The production game still uses its earlier
			unbounded taper; only this study applies the new cutoff. Choose 750 or
			1200 and restart: forward-facing engines should show zero contribution,
			while a turn can restore it.
		</p>
		<p class="invitation">
			<strong
				>Does a shrinking plume make the engine’s declining contribution clear,
				and does its growth through a turn feel right?</strong
			>
			This mapping is exploratory; the plume lifecycle from 02.3 is accepted.
		</p>
	</section>
	<section aria-labelledby="readiness">
		<h2 id="readiness">03 / Readiness — good enough to work with in alpha</h2>
		<p>
			The study gives us a workable direction: angled engines emit glowing
			surfaces that keep drifting and fading after release. Their size follows
			engine output. We can build this with the existing Canvas rendering and
			flight systems; the examples already demonstrate the essential effect.
		</p>
		<p>
			The game currently draws a separate flame and a line trail. The practical
			work is to replace those with the studied effect and connect its size to
			engine output. The approximate 750 cutoff currently lives in the example,
			so the game’s engine calculation also needs that rule if we want the same
			behaviour there. A shared constant is enough to start; this does not
			require a new settings system.
		</p>
		<p>
			<strong
				>This is an alpha working direction, not a specification of the eventual
				product.</strong
			>
			The cutoff, taper, plume size, angle, and lifetime can change as we use
			it. We do not need to settle every detail first. The current directional
			cutoff is a starting interpretation, not a promise of a total-speed
			ceiling. Normal-scale readability and how the effect feels among planets
			still need actual play.
		</p>
	</section>
	<section aria-labelledby="integration">
		<h2 id="integration">
			04 / Integration — the smallest useful implementation
		</h2>
		<p>
			The plan is to make the effect usable in ordinary flight and easy to
			adjust. These are proposed implementation steps; no game behaviour is
			changed by this document.
		</p>
		<ol>
			<li>
				<strong>Reuse the effect that worked.</strong>
				Move the complete fading surfaces from the study into the shared flight
				rendering, replacing the separate flame and line trail. Keep the angled
				emissions and natural fade after release.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Connect appearance to engine output.</strong>
				Use the engine’s contribution to size each plume and bring the
				approximate 750 rule into the shared engine calculation. Start with the
				study’s values as simple constants. Avoid deriving engine output from
				velocity changes caused by gravity.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Make it behave coherently during play.</strong>
				Age and remove surfaces with simulation time, freeze them on pause,
				clear them on reset, and avoid streaks across arena wraps. Keep the Lab
				and game on the same effect code so adjustments carry over.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Fly it and adjust.</strong>
				Check straight flight, turns, release, and the cutoff in the actual
				scene, with focused correctness checks for the changed behaviour. Look
				for distracting glow, disappearing detail, or obvious performance
				problems. Record what we implemented and what remains provisional; use
				the result to guide the next change.
			</li>
		</ol>
		<p>
			The intended result is a useful alpha version we can judge through play.
			More configuration, a general effects framework, and final tuning are not
			prerequisites. Preserve the study examples so we can compare changes
			against the visual quality we liked.
		</p>
	</section>
	<aside aria-label="Study evaluation">
		<p>
			<a href="/lab/reviews/engine-effects"
				>Separate study-quality evaluation</a
			>
			— assesses the evidence and usefulness of this study, not implementation
			readiness or permission to execute the plan.
		</p>
	</aside>
</StudyLayout>
<style>
.invitation {
	border-left: 2px solid var(--color-acid-lime);
	padding-left: var(--spacing-md);
}
</style>
